{"name":"Meringue","hex":"#f3e4b3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"meringue","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mixture consisting of beaten egg whites and either sugar or other flavorings; it is often baked to brown the surface and may be used as a topping or in other ways, most often as a dessert.","A shell made of this mixture which serves as the receptacle for fruit, ice cream or sherbet."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To prepare as a meringue dish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/meringue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Meringue","blocks":[{"text":"Meringue is a type of dessert or candy, traditionally made from whipped egg whites and sugar, and occasionally an acidic ingredient such as lemon, vinegar, or cream of tartar. A binding agent such as salt, flour, or gelatin may also be added to the eggs. The key to the formation of a good meringue is the formation of stiff peaks by denaturing the protein ovalbumin via mechanical shear."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Meringues_9027.jpg/330px-Meringues_9027.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meringue","wikidata":"Q276276"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg/500px-Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg/1280px-Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg/500px-Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Home-made meringues","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHomemade_meringues_(16961016019).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Blancs_d%27oeufs_battus.jpg/500px-Blancs_d%27oeufs_battus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Blancs_d%27oeufs_battus.jpg/500px-Blancs_d%27oeufs_battus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Beaten egg whites","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlancs_d'oeufs_battus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Weeping_meringue_after_refrigeration.png/500px-Weeping_meringue_after_refrigeration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Weeping_meringue_after_refrigeration.png/500px-Weeping_meringue_after_refrigeration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Beads of golden liquid can form on meringue when it is overcooked, undercooked, stored in a refrigerator, or made on a humid day.[10]  This is called weeping or sweating.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWeeping_meringue_after_refrigeration.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Pavlova_dessert.JPG/330px-Pavlova_dessert.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Pavlova_dessert.JPG/960px-Pavlova_dessert.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Pavlova_dessert.JPG/330px-Pavlova_dessert.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APavlova_dessert.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg/500px-Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg/960px-Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg/500px-Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Three_meringue_mushrooms_standing_on_a_table.jpg/500px-Three_meringue_mushrooms_standing_on_a_table.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Three_meringue_mushrooms_standing_on_a_table.jpg/500px-Three_meringue_mushrooms_standing_on_a_table.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Meringue mushrooms","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThree_meringue_mushrooms_standing_on_a_table.jpg"}],"sounds":{"meringue":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/64/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-meringue.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/64/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-meringue.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-meringue.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/64/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-meringue.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-meringue.wav.ogg"}]}}}